      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "The possible color combinations that Terry and Mary can get are: (both red, both blue, one red and one blue):\n- Both have two reds (i.e. RR) or both have two blues (i.e. BB). There are $\\binom{20}{2}=190$ favorable ways for this.\n- One red and one blue (i.e. RB and BR) There are 20 choices for the red and 10 choices for the blue. So there are $20(10)=200$ favorable ways for this.\nNote that both of these events are mutually exclusive from one another. Assuming that Terry and Mary draw from the same container, then the events RRRBBB and BBBRBB are equally likely by symmetry. Hence, the probability can be expressed as $300/(190+190+200)=3/19,$ and thus $19+3=\\boxed{22.}$\n[asy]\nsize(200);\nlabel(\"$r_1$\", (-15,12), S);\nlabel(\"r_2\", (-15,6), S);\nlabel(\"b_1\", (-15,0), S);\nlabel(\"b_2\", (-15,-6), S);\ndraw((0,2)--(-3,0)--(3,0), arrow=Arrow);\ndraw((1.5,1.5)--(5,2), arrow=Arrow);\ndraw((2,3)--(1,0)--(3,0), arrow=Arrow);\ndraw((0,3)--(5,0), arrow=Arrow);\ndraw((3,1.5)--(7.5,-1), arrow=Arrow);\nlabel(\"Terry\", (-15,4), NW);\nlabel(\"$m/n$ is\", (7.5,-1), SW);\nlabel(\"Mary\", (2,3), NE);\n[/asy]. The answer is 22",
